<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=radiocarbon-dating">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radiocarbon dating</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as carbon dating, carbon-14 dating</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radiocarbon dating is a method for determining the age of an object containing organic material by using radiocarbon, a radioactive isotope of carbon. It was developed in the late 1940s at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> by Willard Libby, who proposed in a paper of 1946 that living matter might include 14C. He tested the method on samples of known age, including two Egyptian kings' tombs, in results published in Science in December 1949. The half-life of 14C is about 5,730 years, so the oldest dates reliably measured are approximately 50,000 years ago. Libby received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Chemistry for this work in 1960.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Excavation and <b>radiocarbon dating</b> show that people settled there in the 9th century, building a town of earth-and-timber houses whose inhabitants fished, farmed and traded along the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…as the language of a large epigraphic culture: thousands of inscriptions on stone and bronze survive, and everyday letters and contracts were also written in a cursive script on palm-leaf stalks, many of which have been recovered; <b>radiocarbon dating</b> places the earliest in the early first millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pyu did not vanish with those 3,000: no site shows a violent overthrow, and <b>radiocarbon dating</b> puts human activity at Halin until about 870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Radiocarbon dating</b> then suggested the first metalworking c. 2000–1700 BCE, with rice phytoliths from the lowest grave dated to 2100 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ban-chiang-culture">Ban Chiang culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>He was buried with over 100 stone and bone artefacts, all stained with red ochre, and <b>radiocarbon dating</b> of the skeleton and the bone tools places the burial at about 12,990 to 12,840 years ago.</blockquote><a href="/culture/clovis-culture">Clovis culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Erlitou culture was an early Bronze Age society of the Yellow River valley, c. 1750–1500 BCE by <b>radiocarbon dating</b>, centred on a palace city at Erlitou in Henan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
